--- title
# LOGV Learn — Courses, Enrollment and Billing

--- body
A Next.js course platform bringing content management, progress tracking, authentication and paid access together.

--- actions
[Back to projects](/en/#products) · [Português](/projects/pt/logv-learn/)

:::

::: section architecture
## System boundary and architecture

Next.js App Router serves learner/admin pages and server actions. Prisma/PostgreSQL defines users, courses, modules, lessons, purchases and progress. Clerk manages authentication and an Asaas integration provisions payment requests; incoming provider webhook events attempt to grant or revoke access. React/TanStack Query components implement lesson playback, comments and backoffice screens.
:::

::: section implementation
## Implementation choices

The platform separates course authoring and learner progression into server actions; API route handlers own payment and authentication callbacks. This simplifies a compact monolithic deployment, but payment lifecycle and access control must be treated as an independently testable domain.
:::

::: section alternatives
## Alternatives and trade-offs

A hosted LMS reduces operational effort but limits product-specific workflows. An external billing entitlement service can improve isolation; keeping purchase rows in Prisma is simpler for an early application. PostgreSQL transactions and idempotency constraints are essential when asynchronous webhooks modify enrollment.
:::

::: section testing
## Evidence and concrete defect

The inspected tree contains `src/actions/course-progress.ts`, `src/actions/payment.ts`, `src/app/api/webhooks/asaas/route.ts`, and `prisma/schema.prisma`. No project test files were identified in the tree. In the Asaas handler the comparison string `PAYMET_RECEIVED` differs from the switch case `PAYMENT_RECEIVED`; this appears to be a reachable logic defect, subject to test confirmation. Code presence is not evidence of payment correctness.
:::

::: section limitations
## Known limits and next validation

Focus on webhook signature validation, replay protection, paid enrollment authorization, refund semantics and idempotency under simultaneous deliveries. Add unit and integration tests for PIX and card cases, grant-on-confirmation, refund rollback, duplicate deliveries and database failures. The package build command uses the Windows `set` shell syntax and should be made cross-platform for Linux CI.
:::

::: section evidence
## Source inventory

`src/app/api/webhooks/asaas/route.ts`; `src/actions/payment.ts`; `src/actions/course-progress.ts`; `src/app/(with-layout)/admin/`; `prisma/schema.prisma`; `package.json`.
:::

